Zoom homogeneous box with GEAR sinks
=====================================

What it is
----------

The gas box of examples/SinkParticles/HomogeneousBox, embedded at the centre
of a larger periodic box and run with the zoom region (--zoom). The example
offers a non-cosmological run (default) and a cosmological run (run.sh
variable cosmological=1, makeIC.py option --cosmological, see "Cosmological
ICs").

- Gas box: side L, uniform random gas (PartType0), 50 Msun per particle,
  density 0.1 atom/cm^3, zero velocity, internal energy u = sigma^2 with the
  velocity dispersion of the original example (Jeans wavelength lJ L).
- Dark matter in the gas box (PartType1): one particle per gas particle.
  Each pair comes from one random point split as in space_generate_gas()
  (src/space.c), so gas and dark matter follow the same density field. The
  dark matter density is dm_ratio times the gas density (default 5.36, the
  Planck 2018 ratio of cold dark matter to baryons).
- Background dark matter (PartType2): nested cube shells around the gas box.
  Shell k spans the cube sides 2^(k-1) L to 2^k L, up to the box side
  embed L. Each shell is a jittered grid with spacing 2^k times the mean gas
  spacing, so the particle mass grows by 8 from one shell to the next and
  every shell holds 7/8 of 2^(3 level) particles. No PartType2 particle is
  inside the gas box.
- By default the total matter density outside equals the one inside
  (delta = 0): the large box is uniform. With delta > 0 the gas box is an overdensity
  rho_in / rho_out = 1 + delta, with the profile
  rho(r) = rho_out + (rho_in - rho_out) (1 - tanh((r - L/2) / w))
  of the background dark matter, where r is the max-norm distance from the
  centre. The gas stays a sharp cube.

The resolution is set by level: 2^(3 level) gas particles. The gas box side
grows as 2^level, the mean spacing (27.2 pc) and h (33.6 pc) do not change.

  level  gas = DM particles  PartType2 particles  L [pc]  box [pc] (embed 8)
  3      512                 1344                 218     1744
  4      4096                10752                436     3488
  5      32768               86016                872     6976
  8      16777216            44040192             6976    55805

Configure
---------

./configure --disable-mpi --with-chemistry=GEAR_10 --with-cooling=grackle_0 \
  --with-stars=GEAR --with-star-formation=GEAR --with-feedback=GEAR \
  --with-sink=GEAR --with-kernel=wendland-C2 --with-grackle=path/to/grackle

Sinks do not run with MPI, so build and run without MPI.

Run
---

run.sh has three cases (variable run_case, default level5). Each case makes
the ICs, then runs swift. Add with_star_formation=1 for GEAR star formation
instead of sinks.

1. Production, level 5 (default): 32768 gas particles, 8 threads, time_end 0.5.

     ./run.sh
     with_star_formation=1 ./run.sh

   Sink formation (params_sinks.yml, GEARSink) and star formation
   (params_star_formation.yml, GEARStarFormation) use the thresholds of
   examples/SinkParticles/HomogeneousBox/params.yml: gas density above
   10 atom/cm^3, temperature below 1000 K (sinks) or 3e4 K (stars), star
   formation efficiency 0.01. In that example (gas only, grackle_0) the
   first sinks form at t = 0.26. Here the dark matter makes the box
   collapse faster (free-fall time 65 Myr instead of 163 Myr), so the
   first sinks are expected before t = 0.26. This is not measured.

2. Local test: level 3, 2 threads, time_end 0.1 (98 Myr).

     run_case=local ./run.sh
     run_case=local with_star_formation=1 ./run.sh

   It takes a few seconds on a laptop (release build). The run stops at
   t = 0.1, before the crash given in "Expected behaviour". No sink or star
   forms in this time.

3. Large region, level 8 (16.8 million gas particles), 72 threads.

     run_case=level8 ./run.sh

   This case is for a cluster node (for example Jed). It writes restart
   files every 2 hours and a snapshot every 0.05 (49 Myr).
   - ICs: makeIC.py --level 8 takes 2 minutes, 6.9 GB of memory and
     writes a 3.7 GB file (measured on a laptop).
   - Particles: 16777216 gas (50 Msun), 16777216 PartType1 (268 Msun),
     3 x 14680064 PartType2 (2544, 2.035e4 and 1.628e5 Msun): 77.6 million.
   - Zoom grid: 5 background cells (11.2 kpc), zoom depth 5 (32^3 zoom
     cells of 349 pc), mesh_side_length 512 (mesh cell 109 pc, as at
     level 5).
   - Memory estimate: 19.7 GB for the particles (part, xpart and gpart:
     680 bytes per gas particle, 136 bytes per dark matter particle), so
     about 30 to 40 GB with cells, tasks, spare particles and the mesh.
   - Sinks do not run with MPI: one rank, all cores of one node.
   - Cost estimate: a local level-4 run reached t = 0.055 in 46 core
     seconds. Linear scaling in the particle number (x 4096) and in time
     (x 10 to time_end) gives about 2e6 core seconds, that is 7 hours on
     72 cores if the scaling is perfect. Deeper time-step bins in the
     clumps can make it 3 to 10 times longer. These are estimates, not
     measurements.
   The gas temperature grows as L^2 at fixed jeans_length (makeIC.py
   --lJ, in gas box units): 16 K at level 5, about 1000 K at level 8. Use
   jeans_length=0.03125 to keep the level-5 temperature at level 8.
   The gas cools during the run (cooling is on), so the temperature set by
   jeans_length is only the starting value.

Other run.sh variables: level, n_threads, gas_mass, embed, dm_ratio, delta,
jeans_length, cosmological. gas_mass (Msun, default 50) scales SPH:h_max,
the softenings and the sink cut-off radius by (gas_mass / 50)^(1/3). Run "python3 makeIC.py --help" for all IC
options.

Parameter files: params_sinks.yml (with --sinks) and
params_star_formation.yml (with --star-formation). The physics blocks are
the ones of examples/SinkParticles/HomogeneousBox/params.yml.

Zoom grid and mesh
------------------

makeIC.py writes zoom_runtime_params.txt, which run.sh passes with -P:
- ZoomRegion:bkg_top_level_cells: the largest odd number not above
  embed / pad_factor, so that the padded gas box sits in the central
  background cell.
- ZoomRegion:zoom_top_level_depth: the largest depth for which the zoom
  cells stay wider than 1.1 kernel_gamma SPH:h_max, with at most
  max_zoom_cdim (32) zoom cells per axis.
- Gravity:mesh_side_length: 2^(level+1), at least 32.
With the defaults (embed 8, pad 1.3, h_max 0.08 kpc) this gives 5 background
cells and 174 pc wide zoom cells up to level 7 (depth level - 2), and
349 pc wide zoom cells at level 8. If you change SPH:h_max in the parameter
file, give the same value to makeIC.py --h-max.

Softening
---------

Gas, stars, sinks and PartType1: 5 pc (max_physical_baryon_softening and
max_physical_DM_softening). PartType2: softening_ratio_background (0.2)
times the mean spacing of particles of its mass at the mean matter density
of the box. Without cosmology, the mean matter density is the total matter
mass over the box volume (src/gravity_properties.c). makeIC.py prints the
softening of each shell.

Cosmological ICs
----------------

makeIC.py --cosmological writes comoving ICs (lengths divided by a) and
a_begin is chosen such that the comoving mean
matter density equals Omega_m rho_crit_0, which SWIFT checks at start-up;
Omega_b / Omega_m = 1 / (1 + dm_ratio). With the defaults this gives
a_begin = 0.0136 (z = 72). The velocities are zero, which means the box
follows the Hubble flow: it expands and the physics is not that of the
non-cosmological run. SWIFT stops on zero velocities in cosmological runs,
so these ICs get random velocities (--velocity-sigma, default 1 km/s).
makeIC.py also writes the Cosmology block, the comoving softenings, the
comoving SPH:h_max and the output times to zoom_runtime_params.txt, so the
same parameter files run with --cosmology added to the swift command.

Expected behaviour
------------------

Measured locally (level 3 and 4, release build, 2 threads, a few minutes).
Level 5 and level 8 runs were not made.

- The background softening is set without cosmology: PartType2 softenings
  10.9, 21.8 and 43.6 pc for the three shells, as makeIC.py predicts. A
  binary without this fix stops at the first step (zero time-step of the
  PartType2 particles).
- The time-step stays above zero. The dark matter velocities grow to about
  3 km/s at t = 0.06 (59 Myr) at level 3 and level 4 alike.
- The run does not reproduce the fragmentation of the original example.
  With cold dark matter the total density is 0.636 m_p/cm^3 and the
  free-fall time is about 65 Myr. The whole uniform medium, inside and
  outside the gas box, collapses into clumps on that time scale:
  high-resolution particles leave the gas box (rmax = 1.3 L at t = 0.11 at
  level 3) and PartType2 particles enter it (15 to 49 at t = 0.06 to 0.11).
- The zoom extent regrid then fires (level 4, t = 0.055) and the run stops
  with a segmentation fault in space_reorder_extra_sparts_mapper (level 3
  at t = 0.118, level 4 at t = 0.055). time_end = 0.5 is therefore far
  beyond what runs today. It is the value of the original example and was
  not tuned.
- Cosmological ICs (--cosmological, 20 steps): the run starts and ends
  without error.

What is not tested
------------------

- Builds with debugging checks stop at the first rebuild:
  - with --sinks, in the sink cell index check (space_rebuild.c), even
    before any sink forms: the spare sink slots of the zoom cells get an
    index from the zoom grid, the check uses the plain grid;
  - with --star-formation, in cell_check_multipole on a background cell
    with one particle. params_star_formation.yml sets
    Scheduler:cell_extra_sinks to 0, which avoids the sink check.
- Sink formation: no sink formed in the local runs.
- The collapse time, the time of the first sinks and the cost to time_end.
- MPI: sinks do not run with MPI.
- The cosmological physics (only start-up and 20 steps were run).
- Level 5 and above (only the ICs and the level-5 zoom geometry).
